{
 "thread": "CB7SnisEpPI",
 "subject": "Confused trying to implement Multinomial - Logistic Normal Mixed Effects Model",
 "messages_listed": 4,
 "last_activity": "2018-02-08T22:17:05Z",
 "messages": [
  {
   "id": "F9ICf01ZCAAJ",
   "from": "Justin Silverman",
   "author": "e7014f74b5d49111",
   "date": "2018-02-07T20:18:31Z",
   "subject": "Confused trying to implement Multinomial - Logistic Normal Mixed Effects Model",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"text-align:center;clear:both\"></p>Hi All, <div><br></div><div>I an new to R-INLA and am having a hell of a time trying to figure out how to code a specific model. </div><div><br></div><div>I have data in the form of a matrix of counts Y where Y_ij represents the counts assigned to category j in sample i. </div><div>N is the total number of rows (samples). </div><div>D is the total number of categories (i.e., Y has D columns)</div><div>I also have two vectors of length N (X and Z) which are both categorical (e.g., they are vectors of integers with Q and M categories respectively). </div><div><br></div><div>I would like to fit the following model (just showing the likelihood):</div><div><br></div><p style=\"text-align:center;clear:both\"><a href=\"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-Eo522WsMpfQ/Wntc414syyI/AAAAAAAAA9c/SGozP2E8C9w29U_8LZ18sC64DzpBA9MCgCLcBGAs/s1600/Y_i_%2526_sim_text_M.png\" style=\"margin-left:1em;margin-right:1em\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-Eo522WsMpfQ/Wntc414syyI/AAAAAAAAA9c/SGozP2E8C9w29U_8LZ18sC64DzpBA9MCgCLcBGAs/s1600/Y_i_%252526_sim_text_M.png&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792443626035000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0jQT_HXHgw4Rl_wycMIbsW\"><img src=\"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-Eo522WsMpfQ/Wntc414syyI/AAAAAAAAA9c/SGozP2E8C9w29U_8LZ18sC64DzpBA9MCgCLcBGAs/s320/Y_i_%2526_sim_text_M.png\" border=\"0\" width=\"320\" height=\"206\"></a></p><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\">Here Y_i refers to the i-th row of Y, π_i is a vector satisfying sum(π_i) = 1, \\phi^-1 represents a softmax transform (or really a bijective softmax - where one element is set to zero for identifiability). Under this transformation (\\phi) π_i is transformed to the D-1 dimensional vector η_i. β^(X_i)  and γ^(Z_i) represent D-1 dimensional vectors corresponding to the random coefficients for the factors X and Z respectively. Thus β is a Q x (D-1) matrix and γ is a M x (D-1) matrix. In addition there is added random noise in the form of ϵ which is sample specific (again ϵ_i is a D-1 dimensional vector).  </p><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\"><br></p><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\">Is it possible to infer this model in R-INLA? I have read through the FAQ here (<a href=\"http://www.r-inla.org/faq#TOC-I-have-multinomial-data-but-I-cannot-find-the-multinomial-likelihood-isn-t-it-available-\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.r-inla.org/faq%23TOC-I-have-multinomial-data-but-I-cannot-find-the-multinomial-likelihood-isn-t-it-available-&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792443626035000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1OtNlqal-iaMJ1t_kBhr9y\">http://www.r-inla.org/faq#TOC-I-have-multinomial-data-but-I-cannot-find-the-multinomial-likelihood-isn-t-it-available-</a>) but this does not go over how to write this model for a categorical predictor (e.g., X and Z) where they end up being matricies (which in the Poisson formulation have rows that must sum to zero). I tried to get my head around the alli multinomial example but I must say I found it extremely confusing. Any help would be much appreciated. </p><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\"><br></p><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\">Thank you!</p><p style=\"text-align:left;clear:both\">Justin Silverman</p><div><br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "tRHaU31ZCAAJ",
   "from": "Justin Silverman",
   "author": "e7014f74b5d49111",
   "date": "2018-02-07T20:21:56Z",
   "subject": "Re: Confused trying to implement Multinomial - Logistic Normal Mixed Effects Model",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Here is what I have so far:<div><br></div><div><div>idx = rep(1:n, times = d)</div><div>jdx = rep(1:d, each=n)</div><div>Y.inla &lt;- c(as.matrix(Y))</div><div>X.inla &lt;- rep(X[,1], times=d)</div><div>Z.inla &lt;- rep(Z[,1], times=d)</div><div><br></div><div>formula = y ~</div><div>  ## I believe the following is required to ensure equality between the multinomial </div><div>  ## formulation and the poisson formulation</div><div>  f(idx, model = &quot;iid&quot;, hyper=list(prec = list(initial = log(0.000001), fixed=TRUE))) </div></div><div><br></div><div>After that I am really not very sure... I realize this is not a great start... Thank you so much!</div><div>Justin</div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
