tyle=\"color:#000\">initial </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> log</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.01</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">fixed</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">TRUE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  f</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">gamma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> constr</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">FALSE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> hyper</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">list</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">prec </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> list</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">initial </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> log</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.001</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">fixed</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">TRUE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#800\">##  If the initial precision for epsilon is too low then model will fit terribly</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  f</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">epsilon</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> constr </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> FALSE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> hyper</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">list</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">prec </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> list</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">initial </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> log</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">fixed</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">TRUE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br><br></span><span style=\"color:#800\"># Linear Combinations</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>fit </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\">  inla</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">formula</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> data </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> Y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">inla</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> family </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;poisson&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>            control</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">predictor </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> list</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">compute</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">TRUE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> <br>            control</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">compute</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">list</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">config</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">TRUE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>sam </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> inla</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">posterior</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">sample</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2000</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> fit</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></div></code></div><div><br></div></div><div><br></div><div>My big question now is about the prior specification. Currently I pretty much have independent priors for every parameter in my model. In particular, I am a little worried about the ~ 600 (N x D) independent priors for epsilon terms when really there should only be ~ 50 (D x D)/2 in the form of the covariance matrix V. While it may not be so bad with only 60 samples, my real datasets have thousands and I worry this will get out of hand if it is scaling as N x D (plus its not really using the information in the data optimally). </div><div><br></div><div>Is there some way to rectify this situation and model the epsilons as sharing a covariance structure? I know there is a Wishart prior that goes up to 5 dimensions but it seems to stop there. Even if I modeled them as having diagonal covariance it would be better because at least then its sharing information across samples. </div></div>"
